    id: "f3140b4dcbb3619b517f",
    cat: "ai",
    title: "Anthropic flags revenue surge to investors ahead of stock market debut",
    summary: "It comes as some investors have become concerned about the amount of money being spent on AI infrastructure.",
    preview: "Artificial intelligence (AI) giant Anthropic saw its revenue surge by 45% to $16.8bn (£12.7bn) in the three months to the end of September, the BBC understands. The figures were revealed by the Claude-developer in a briefing for prospective investors on Wednesday as it lays the groundwork for a stock market listing, which is expected to take place before the end of the year.",
    body: "Artificial intelligence (AI) giant Anthropic saw its revenue surge by 45% to $16.8bn (£12.7bn) in the three months to the end of September, the BBC understands.\n\nThe figures were revealed by the Claude-developer in a briefing for prospective investors on Wednesday as it lays the groundwork for a stock market listing, which is expected to take place before the end of the year.\n\nIt comes as some tech stocks, including firms that make the chips and other hardware that power data centres, have fallen in recent days over concerns about the hundreds of billions of dollars being spent on AI infrastructure.\n\nAn Anthropic spokesperson declined to comment.\n\nAnthropic's revenue has risen in successive quarters this year, from $4.7bn in the first three months to $11.6bn the following period.\n\nThe company also reportedly told investors during the event at its headquarters in San Francisco that it had made a profit for the second quarter in a row.\n\nIts revenue figures, along with those of rival OpenAI, offer an important gauge of the AI industry's growth and ability to finance the infrastructure needed to develop the technology.\n\nStocks in some US technology companies fell on Wednesday over concerns around investments in AI.\n\nShare in chip makers - including AMD, Nvidia and Intel - fell sharply.\n\nIn Asia, shares in Japanese tech investment firm Softbank fell by more than 9% on Thursday morning. South Korean semiconductor giants were also down, with Samsung falling by 9.4% and SK Hynix dropping 10.8%.\n\nTech-heavy indexes in the region were also down. Trading on South Korea's Kospi and Kosdaq was temporarily halted after falling by more than 8%, while Japan's Nikkei 225 fell by over 5%.\n\n- How backlash against data centres could shape the US midterm elections\n\n- Published8 hours ago\n\n- 'I applied for 200 jobs' - How 'spray and pray' AI job applications can backfire\n\n- Published22 hours ago\n\nShares have been falling this week after remarks by Hock Tan, the chief executive of US tech manufacturer Broadcom, over AI spending.\n\nTan said at a conference in Singapore on Monday that many investors have \"no clue\" and have set \"extreme expectations only to find out that it's almost like they are in a casino\".\n\nThe falls deepened after US inflation data released on Wednesday showed price rises accelerated by more than expected in September.\n\nInvestors are concerned that higher inflation will mean the US Federal Reserve is more likely to put up the cost of borrowing.\n\nThere also been concerns over the safety of AI models.\n\nOpenAI chief executive Sam Altman said in September that his company will not go public this year as it addresses concerns about the technology's safety.\n\nThe ChatGPT-developer is also eyeing a bumper stock market listing, which is expected to value the firm at more than $1tn.\n\nAnthropic has also been vocal about AI's risks, with its chief executive Dario Amodei calling on the pace of development to slow down.\n\nThe company has continued expanding globally, opening an office in Singapore on Wednesday.",
